from ../structure import StructuralContext

export anchor Tokens:
    description:
        A token is one piece of a statement that analysis treats as a unit: a
        word, a number, a punctuation mark. Tokenizing is simply cutting a
        statement into these pieces, and it follows fixed rules about where
        one piece ends and the next begins.

    kinds:
        ```text
        word           letters, digits, hyphens and apostrophes     disabled, submit-label
        possessive     the 's ending, split off its word            button 's
        number         digits, with an optional unit after them     300, 1.5, 300ms
        quoted         text between matching quotes                 "Save"
        code           text between backticks                       `piton build`
        reference      a Piton reference written into the prose     @{Button}
        punctuation    one mark                                     , . ; : ? !
        symbol         any other single character                   = < > +
        ```

    referencesAreSpecial:
        A Piton reference is one token, however long, and it is already
        resolved: the compiler knows exactly what @\{Button} points at. This is
        the most direct way prose can connect to structure, as described in
        @{StructuralContext}, and analysis never second-guesses it.

    quotedAndCodeAreOpaque:
        Quoted text and code are single tokens that analysis does not look
        inside. They are values to be kept exactly, not words to interpret:
        the label is "Save" means the label is exactly Save.

    position:
        Every token remembers the line and column where it starts in the
        source file, so that anything built from it can say where it came
        from.

    example:
        ```text
        When clicked, its action is invoked.

        When | clicked | , | its | action | is | invoked | .
        ```
